{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Reefs experiencing higher interannual variability in sea surface temperature (SST) exhibit greater year‐to‐year turnover in fish species composition (higher temporal beta diversity).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether reefs with more variable SST across years have higher temporal community turnover.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef, compute the interannual standard deviation of mean_sst over all surveyed years.  \n2. For each reef, compute year‐to‐year Bray–Curtis dissimilarity (or Jaccard distance) in species composition, averaging pairwise dissimilarities between consecutive years to get a temporal beta diversity metric.  \n3. Fit a linear regression or generalized least squares model: temporal_beta_diversity ~ sd_mean_sst + protection_status + (1|region).  \n4. Assess the strength and significance of the association between SST variability and beta diversity (e.g., via mixed‐effects model p‐values or permutation tests).  \n5. Visualize the relationship with a scatterplot of beta diversity vs. sd_mean_sst, colored by protection_status, with fitted trend lines.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of per‐reef sd_mean_sst and temporal beta diversity.  \n2. Model summary showing effect size and significance of sd_mean_sst.  \n3. Scatterplot with trend lines distinguishing protected vs. unprotected reefs."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Years with anomalously high SST (above the long‐term mean) coincide with an increased proportional contribution of planktivorous fishes to total fish productivity on reefs.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Evaluate whether heat stress shifts community production toward planktivorous functional groups.",  
      "steps":"1. Classify each species in the dataset into functional groups (planktivores vs. non‐planktivores) using the trophic_level or diet column.  \n2. For each reef‐year, sum prod for planktivores and for total community; compute the proportion planktivore_prod_prop = planktivore_prod / total_prod.  \n3. Compute per‐reef long‐term mean of mean_sst, then for each reef‐year calculate SST_anomaly = mean_sst ‐ mean(mean_sst).  \n4. Fit a mixed‐effects model: planktivore_prod_prop ~ SST_anomaly + protection_status + (1|region), using a binomial or beta regression framework (e.g. glmmTMB).  \n5. Test whether SST_anomaly has a positive coefficient.  \n6. Plot planktivore_prod_prop against SST_anomaly with separate smoothing lines by protection_status.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of reef‐year SST_anomaly and planktivore production proportion.  \n2. Mixed‐effects model output testing SST anomaly effect.  \n3. Plot of planktivore_prod_prop vs. SST_anomaly annotated by protection_status."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Reefs surveyed across a single depth stratum exhibit stronger negative sensitivity of total fish productivity to SST anomalies than reefs with two depth strata.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare SST‐productivity sensitivity between reefs with one vs. two depth strata.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef, determine the number of distinct depth_stratum values in the survey (one or two).  \n2. For each reef, fit a simple linear regression of total_prod ~ mean_sst across all years, extracting the slope (sensitivity).  \n3. Compile per‐reef slopes and stratum_count (1 vs. 2).  \n4. Use a t‐test or ANCOVA: slope ~ stratum_count + (1|region) (via mixed model) to test if single‐stratum reefs have more negative slopes.  \n5. Generate a boxplot comparing slope distributions by stratum_count with overlaid significance annotation.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of per‐reef SST‐productivity sensitivity slopes and stratum counts.  \n2. Statistical test (mixed model or t‐test) comparing slopes between one‐ and two‐stratum reefs.  \n3. Boxplot of slopes by stratum_count."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Production‐to‐biomass (P/B) ratios of fish communities decrease with increasing latitude (i.e., northern reefs have lower P/B than southern reefs).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess how community turnover rates (P/B) vary along the latitudinal gradient of the Gulf of California.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef‐year, compute total production (sum prod) and total standing biomass (sum biomass), then derive PB_ratio = total_prod / total_biomass.  \n2. Compile reef‐year records with PB_ratio and latitude (degree).  \n3. Fit a linear mixed‐effects model: PB_ratio ~ latitude + protection_status + (1|region).  \n4. Check for a negative effect of latitude on PB_ratio.  \n5. Visualize PB_ratio vs. latitude with a trend line and 95% confidence band, separated by protection_status.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Data frame of reef‐year PB_ratio and latitude.  \n2. Mixed‐effects model summary testing latitude effect.  \n3. Scatterplot of PB_ratio vs. latitude with fitted lines."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Reefs with greater functional trait diversity in body size (larger range between smallest and largest fish) support higher mean annual fish productivity, after controlling for standing biomass.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Determine whether structural trait diversity (size range) enhances overall productivity independent of biomass amount.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef‐year, compute body_size_range = max(length_cm) − min(length_cm) across all individuals/slides.  \n2. Also compute total_prod and total_biomass.  \n3. Fit a multiple regression or mixed model: total_prod ~ body_size_range + total_biomass + protection_status + (1|region).  \n4. Test whether body_size_range has a significant positive coefficient when biomass is accounted for.  \n5. Plot partial residuals of productivity vs. body_size_range controlling for biomass.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of reef‐year body_size_range, total_prod, total_biomass.  \n2. Model output showing effect of size range on productivity.  \n3. Partial residual plot of productivity against size range."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Reefs within marine protected areas that have been protected for longer periods exhibit steeper positive trends in fish biomass over time compared to newer or unprotected reefs.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Evaluate whether protection duration increases biomass recovery rates.",  
      "steps":"1. For each protected reef, derive protection_start_year as the earliest survey year labeled as 'protected'; compute protection_duration = year − protection_start_year for each reef‐year.  \n2. For unprotected reefs, set protection_duration = 0.  \n3. For each reef, fit a linear regression of total_biomass ~ year to get biomass_trend_slope.  \n4. Compile per‐reef biomass_trend_slope and protection_duration at last survey year.  \n5. Fit a regression: biomass_trend_slope ~ protection_duration + (1|region).  \n6. Test whether slope increases with longer protection_duration.  \n7. Plot biomass_trend_slope vs. protection_duration with fitted line and region‐level connectors.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of per‐reef biomass trend slopes and protection durations.  \n2. Mixed‐effects model output testing protection_duration effect.  \n3. Scatterplot of trend slopes vs. protection_duration by region."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Mean compositional dissimilarity among reefs (spatial beta diversity) has decreased over the study period, indicating biogeographic homogenization.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test for temporal trends in spatial beta diversity across reefs.",  
      "steps":"1. For each survey year, construct a species presence/absence (or abundance) matrix across all reefs sampled in that year.  \n2. Calculate pairwise Bray–Curtis or Jaccard dissimilarities among reefs and average them to get mean spatial beta diversity per year.  \n3. Compile a time series of mean spatial beta diversity vs. year.  \n4. Fit a time‐series regression: mean_beta_diversity ~ year (e.g. GLS with autocorrelation).  \n5. Test whether the slope is significantly negative.  \n6. Plot mean_beta_diversity against year with trend line and 95% CI.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Time series of mean spatial beta diversity by year.  \n2. Statistical test of temporal trend (slope and p‐value).  \n3. Trend plot of beta diversity over time."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Under high fishing pressure (unprotected reefs), a greater proportional contribution of small cryptobenthic fishes (<10 cm) to total productivity buffers declines in overall productivity compared to reefs dominated by larger species.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess whether cryptobenthic dominance stabilizes productivity on heavily fished reefs.",  
      "steps":"1. Classify individual fish observations as cryptobenthic if length_cm <10, otherwise non‐cryptobenthic.  \n2. For each reef‐year, sum prod for cryptobenthics and total_prod, compute crypt_prop = crypt_prod / total_prod.  \n3. Also record total_prod and protection_status.  \n4. Fit an interaction model: total_prod ~ crypt_prop * protection_status + (1|region), using mixed‐effects regression.  \n5. Test whether the interaction term for crypt_prop × unprotected is positive, indicating buffering.  \n6. Visualize predicted total productivity as a function of crypt_prop separately for protected vs. unprotected reefs.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Reef‐year table with crypt_prop, total_prod, protection_status.  \n2. Mixed‐effects model summary with interaction effects.  \n3. Interaction plot showing productivity vs. cryptobenthic proportion by protection status."  
    }  
  }  
]}
